Relevant columns and their potential usage for this task  
• "block_timestamp": micro‑seconds since Unix epoch; used to locate the exact second a transaction was added to a block, extract month/year, and to measure the exact number of seconds in every month.  
• "transaction_hash": string that identifies the transaction; we count every row that carries a hash (no de‑duplication).  
• "block_number", "log_index", "address", "removed", "block_hash", "transaction_index": not needed for the requested metrics but can help in sanity checks or additional grouping if desired.  
Main filtering condition: limit rows to calendar‑year 2023  
    WHERE "block_timestamp" >= 1672531200000000  -- 2023‑01‑01 00:00:00 UTC, micro‐seconds  
      AND "block_timestamp" <  1704067200000000  -- 2024‑01‑01 00:00:00 UTC, micro‐seconds  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that progress from simple inspection to the complete monthly count + TPS computation.  
All column names are wrapped in double quotes and every query is preceded by a short comment.

```sql
